  {
    "episode_id": 83466,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83466.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. Well, I think back to my very first political memory and Stanford, Connecticut, it's election night. in 1952. We live at that point in the second floor, as they used to say, cold water walk up in my grandmother's house, my mother's mother. and we're watching TV and my dad really it was the first in the neighborhood by TV it was about the size of a bureau and the screen was about the size of my smartphone screen but we watched the returns of the presidential election so my grandmother and I quite naturally it seems to me are going for General Eisenhower. I mean, he's the one who won the Second World War. He stopped the Nazis, you know? Right. And in Europe. And then I see as evening goes on that my mom and dad are going for this guy, Stevenson. And I don't get it. I was too respectful. I attempted to figure it out. But later on, as I reported in the book, I talked to my dad about it a couple of years later. And I said, was it because he always supported the Democrat? Oh, no, I just thought that as great a hero as Ike was, he really didn't have the background to be a great president and Stevenson at the governor and he was smart, all that stuff. But I remember my dad said, listen, you don't know this, but 1940, I voted for Wendell Wilkie, the Republican candidate against Roosevelt because I thought two terms was enough And I thought Wilkie was a really independent-minded, accomplished business leader and internationalist, et cetera, et cetera. So that surprised me. And those lessons stayed with me probably more than you'd imagined as an",
