    "turn_text": "a part of Obamacare. So it's uncomfortable because you're disagreeing with almost all your colleagues in your party caucus, which is a important part of your life. But honestly, you're there to do what you think is right. And to do the best for your country and your constituents. So I know what Joe is going through now. But I also know that his independence on these matters, including his Build Back Better Build, a $1.75 trillion additional spending on social programs at all, is quite sincere and it's quite consistent with the rest of his career going back to West Virginia state government. But he really is a centrist and he is fiscally responsible So he's worried about the impact of the so-called Build Back Better Bill. He has the power to stop it himself, because it's 50-50 in the Senate. And he's used a really excellent word, which is he said he really supports applause. And I think what he's saying there is that not that he's unalterably opposed to what's in the Build Back Better Bill, But he wants to pause it. And I think in parts of it gets more detailed consideration review by partisan involvement than it has so far. But also, he's worried about inflation. And I think he's right to wait and see how we're going to look next year, six months from now or a year from now. How are we going to look? How are we going to look when it comes to the virus? And one person can really stop something or pass it. And I hope Joe Manchin uses the authority he has to press the pause button on this bill. And I would say, if I had to predict, and I really don't know, although we're good friends, Joe and I, that he's going to say pause. I think Gary, that that'll only be good for the country, but I think it'll be good for the Democratic Party, even though the most Democrats don't feel that way, because I think if it passes, it will be a target",
