    "turn_text": "Well, it's possible. You know, I was just at a meeting in Stanford, and oh, there were three or four people around the table who were waiting for somebody else. And it actually started to talk politics. And the others around the table all said they're not comfortable in either political party today. I bet, and the polling seems to suggest that that that actually represents a majority of the American people. But the parties controlled by left and right don't give those choices. Often the parties in the nominating process go left and right for president. But the candidates try to move back toward the center for election day. But if the two parties don't come to the center more than they have, Then I think for the first time in American history since 1860, which is the last time we had a successful third party, which was the Republicans with Abraham Lincoln. In no labels, we try to work through the two major parties to build sort of bipartisan platform for centrist Democrats and centrist Republicans. And it worked. It's worked in a couple of cases on one of the first pandemic response bills in between Trump and Biden coming in. And it worked on the bipartisan infrastructure bill, in which case, the no labels members of Congress really played the central role in both. But honestly, if it doesn't work, I will God on a limb and say that 2024, and it depends who's nominated. If Republicans go again with President Trump or somebody like Trump and the Democrats go further left, there's going to be a lot of people who want a third choice. And it might happen.",
