  {
    "episode_id": 83466,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83466.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 88,
    "turn_text": "Sometimes young people come and ask me about how they should get into politics and what they should expect. What do they need to do? Well, sometimes I usually surprise them by telling them they should read history and biography to get a better sense of the impact that leaders can have on societies that are bad and maybe form a judgment about whether they want to get involved. But I also tell them, and I may not naturally think about this when they're young, that part of what you need to succeed in politics, probably in most things of life, is physical and intellectual stamina. Of course, you need to be persistent because it's hard to work. And the way to succeed is by working hard and not giving up. The second thing, once you get to be a leader is what we talked about earlier. And that is to try to make a difference. I mean, being a leader cannot be just about staying in power. There's got to be a reason you work so hard and use all that stamina to get elected. And it is to make a difference, hopefully, for the better.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
